-Deep learning applied to electroencephalographic (EEG) functional connectivity has become a prominent approach to detecting major depressive disorder (MDD), with reported classification accuracies frequently exceeding 90\%. Recent reviews have examined how these studies partition data into training and test sets, but the connectivity pipeline that produces the model input has not been examined. Connectivity estimation, thresholding, and normalization performed before partitioning can transfer test information into training, and epoch-level partitioning can place segments from the same subject on both sides of the split. We audited \NincludedTotal{} studies applying deep learning to EEG connectivity features for MDD classification, extracting pipeline order, partitioning unit, sample size, and reported performance, and rating each study's leakage risk using explicit criteria applied independently by two raters (agreement $\kappa$ = \Kappa). \PctUnclearAny\% of studies did not report sufficient detail to determine whether preprocessing was confined to the training fold, and \PctEpoch\% partitioned at the epoch rather than subject level. Median reported accuracy was \MedLikely\% among studies rated leakage-likely against \MedFree\% among those rated leakage-free; with only \NaccFree{} leakage-free studies, this difference was not distinguishable from zero. We propose a reporting checklist enabling readers to verify that connectivity pipelines are free of leakage.
