+Deep learning applied to electroencephalographic (EEG) functional connectivity is a prominent approach to detecting major depressive disorder (MDD), with reported accuracies often above 90\%. Recent reviews have examined how these studies split data into training and test sets, but not the connectivity pipeline that produces the model input: connectivity estimation, thresholding, normalization, and channel or band selection performed before partitioning can transfer test information into training, and epoch-level partitioning can place segments from one subject on both sides of the split. We define five such leakage surfaces and a four-level risk rating applied from reported methods alone, and applied it to \NincludedTotal{} studies. Two raters agreed on \PctAgree\% of ratings (Cohen's kappa \Kappa). Of the audited studies, \PctEpoch\% partitioned at the epoch rather than the subject level, and \PctUnclearAny\% left at least one preprocessing step undescribed. No study reported fitting connectivity estimation, thresholds, or normalization on the whole dataset; beyond partitioning, the detectable risk came from selecting channels or bands on all data (\PctWholeSelect\% of studies). Only \NaccFree{} studies rated leakage-free reported an eligible accuracy, too few to test whether accuracy differs by rating. We propose an eight-item reporting checklist that lets a reader verify, from the paper alone, that a connectivity pipeline is free of leakage.
