 \subsection{Clinical motivation}
 Major depressive disorder (MDD) is among the leading causes of disability worldwide \cite{ferrari2013}, yet its diagnosis still rests on clinical interviews and self-report scales, which depend on patient cooperation and clinician expertise and have shown limited test-retest reliability \cite{lin2024,regier2013}. An objective physiological marker would support diagnosis, and electroencephalography (EEG) is an attractive candidate: it is relatively affordable, non-invasive, portable, and resolves neural activity on the millisecond scale \cite{simmatis2023}.
 
-Depression is increasingly understood to involve abnormal communication within and between large-scale brain networks \cite{kaiser2015}. Functional connectivity---the statistical dependence between signals recorded at different sites, estimated with measures such as coherence, the phase-locking value, or the phase lag index---offers a way to capture that communication from scalp EEG. Deep learning has made it practical to learn from connectivity matrices directly, treating them as images for convolutional networks or as graphs for graph neural networks, without hand-selecting network features. A substantial literature now applies these models to distinguishing people with MDD from healthy controls, and many studies report classification accuracies above 90\% \cite{kaur2025}.
